we heard some from anne-marie trevelli  in there about what is happening in  westminster i mean what is going on with  some of these parliamentarians that this  behavior is still going on uh for all  this time  well i want to begin by apologizing for  my i.t problems here in the uh  uh you think we'd have all mastered it  by now but to come to your question  uh i think it's been a bad week for  politics uh  you know these things seem  out of time  and women today whether it's in politics  or journalism or any other  workplace  are clearly not going to put up with  the kind of language and behavior that  we've seen this week although we might  have seen years and decades ago and if i  had one conclusion from the whole thing  it's that all of this just seems out of  time with the age we're living in it's  2022 it's not  some time  decades ago so these behaviors do no  good for politics and and that matters  because  this is the arena whereby the nation has  to work out its problems that's what  politics is for well that's  on the cost of living crisis or attitude  to the war in ukraine or any other major  question  i mean uh you've been in uh parliament  now since about 2005. it's quite a long  run there have you seen inappropriate  behavior  in the house of parliament  i personally haven't of course you know  these reports are there for  for all to see  and people are there we're there to  represent our constituents we're there  to do  uh the best job we can  and  i still think despite all the  awful reports of  things that we've seen over the past few  days most people who come into politics  do it for good reasons they do it for  public service they do it because they  want to serve their country and serve  their constituents  and that's really important as i say  because  if we conclude from all this but it's  all simply a nest of rogues and bullies  and you know maybe even worse then  that's really bad for our democracy so  we're not asking for  sainthood in politics but we are asking  for proper professional behavior and  some of the instances that we've seen  over the past week clearly fall short of  that standard i mean there are some  rules of course in place and there are  procedures to deal with this sort of  thing are they just simply  not strong enough  well i think your viewers will feel  we've heard all this before about rules  and procedures and they have been  changed in recent years and  you know i believe that the speaker is  serious about this and making it a good  place and a safe place for people to  work and a place where people can raise  things but  it doesn't look so far  like the changes of recent years have  fully dealt with the problem  if it had then you wouldn't be seeing  the reports that we've seen over the  past week  okay let's just move on slightly if we  can you will have seen some of the  reporting for the past  day or so about the leader of your party  uh sakia stammer and those allegations  that he uh broke lockdown rules up in  durham i mean  no  equivalence with what's happened in  downtreat and whitehall of course but if  mr summer is found to have broken the  rules and is fine should he resign  well the police have already looked at  this and done them and they've they've  found nothing to  to take action on and um you know people  are trying to draw some equivalence to  this  but the police have already looked at it  and that's been their conclusion and i  simply don't believe  that  this is equivalent in any way to a  series of parties bring your own booze  boo's been brought in by the suitcase  with abba singing winner takes all in  the background  and the attempt to draw equivalence is  to  is to try to it's always you know  they want people to think  everybody it's all the same so no  scandal matters uh and that's a bad  place for this i'm not sorry i i did  make it very clear i'm not trying to  draw an equivalence at all what i'm  simply saying is that the labour party  have said that boris johnson and rich  should resign if they're found to have  broken the rules and they've been fined  i'm saying that if it does transpire  that secure stamina or anger or anyone  else for that matter the labor party has  broken the rules and is fined should  they resign well that situation hasn't  arisen because the durham police have  looked at this and found that no rules  were broken so  uh that doesn't arise in this case  and if it did just finally  well but it hasn't and it doesn't okay  you refuse  fine refuse to go on that i perfectly  understand let's move on a little bit if  we can then to uh the situation that the  country is facing you will have of  course seen uh reporting that the  chancellor of the exchequer is  suggesting now a windfall tax be brought  in um that's something the labour party  have been calling for hasn't it  uh yes it is something we've been  calling for and for good reason the cost  of living crisis is really hurting  families we've called for this for  about four months now in order to get  extra help particularly to people in the  lowest incomes with these rocketing  energy bills and today is the first day  that people will see the tory tax rises  in their payslips as well as we come to  the end of april  and the chancellor has so far  steadfastly ruled out taking this action  i hope he does reconsider and if he does  it will be  an admission that the spring statement  that he gave a few weeks ago  fell far short of the scale of the  challenge and the scale of help that  people need  i mean apart from the windfall tax what  else would be done obviously this energy  crisis is continuing  on is there anything different that the  labour party would be doing to try and  deal with the cost of living crisis well  apart from the windfall tax we have  called for  uh the elimination of vat on energy  bills which is something that uh the  prime minister and many people who  supported brexit said would be one of  the earliest uh benefits of brexit well  we've got brexit now and now they've  changed their mind and they don't want  to do this so that's also something that  could take  money off bills  and  in terms of the tax rises that are  coming in now we've also said what is  the point  of being the only country in the g7  to ask working people to pay more income  taxes this year  only to promise to reduce them in two  years time taxation should be about the  needs of public services not the timing  of the tory election campaign plan and  that's what the chancellor's doing with  this hoki koki act on income tax so  there's a number of things that could be  done that would get real help to people  with this cost of living crisis right  now  and just a final thought then we found  and on the news this morning they were  reporting that um two brits seem to have  been taken by russian forces in ukraine  i mean what should be done to try and  get those people back and what message  do you think they should be for any  britons who are still in ukraine should  they all just return home now  well i understand that the foreign  office will do everything they can for  any british person uh captured  abroad uh we've seen with the visit of  the un secretary general yesterday  to ukraine where the russians actually  shelled the capital while he was there  the contempt that russia is showing for  the united nations right now  we've got to stand strong against that  do what we can to help ukraine  a country which is engaged in a an  existential fight for its own freedom  and its right to decide its own future  and for all the divisions in politics we  are right behind all our allies in  trying to do that

